.\"	@(#)puts.3s	6.1 (Berkeley) 5/15/85
.\"
.TH PUTS 3S  "August 1, 1992"
.ds ]W ANSI C
.SH NAME
puts, fputs \- put a string on a stream
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.B int puts(const char *\fIs\fR);
.PP
.B int fputs(const char *\fIs\fP, FILE *\fIstream\fR);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Puts
copies the null-terminated string
.I s
to the standard output stream
.B stdout
and appends a
newline character.
.PP
.I Fputs
copies the null-terminated string
.I s
to the named output
.IR stream .
No newline character is appended.
.PP
Neither routine copies the terminal null character.
.SH "RETURN VALUE"
.I Puts
and
.I fputs
returns EOF for unsuccessful calls and stores an error code in
\fIerrno\fR, otherwise it returns zero.
.SH "ERRORS"
The underlying function of
.I puts
and
.I fputs
is \fIwrite\fR(2).
The error conditions specified for \fIwrite\fR(2)
apply to
.I puts
and
.I fputs.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
write(2)
fopen(3S),
gets(3S),
putc(3S),
printf(3S)
